From c63fc4ae4b09698a8294108d98e9a45552e19274 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: qiyinxi <qiyinxi@foxmail.com>
Date: Wed, 11 Nov 2026 23:21:09 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 100/101] MAS local: let the system maximize and snap the lean
 window

The lean window moved and resized itself, without the caption's resizable
frame and maximize box, so that Windows never snapped or maximized it: the
guest screen has a fixed shape and would have been stretched. Now the guest
screen is fitted into a window of another shape and centered, with black
around it, so the window can be handled like any other.

The frame: WS_THICKFRAME and WS_MAXIMIZEBOX are back, the client area still
covers the whole window. A press on the title bar or a resize band goes on
to DefWindowProc, whose move and size loops snap the window at the edges of
the screen, maximize it at the top and maximize or restore it on a double
click; SC_MAXIMIZE, SC_MOVE and SC_SIZE are no longer refused and the system
menu has its usual items again. WM_SIZING keeps the guest screen's shape
while an edge is dragged. A maximized window hangs over the work area by its
frame, which is client area here: the title bar and the guest screen keep
out of that part, and there are no resize bands. The title bar gets no
maximize button.

The contents: the container no longer undoes a maximize. The scroll area
centers the guest screen, and the GPU subwindow follows through the content
offset. When the window was given a size (maximized, snapped, restored,
SetWindowPos) it keeps it and the guest screen is fitted into it, soon after
the resize instead of half a second later; when the guest screen changes on
its own (first show, rotation, the scale shortcuts) the window still takes
its size, unless the system holds the window, and then the guest screen is
fitted again. Clicks on the bars land on the scroll area's viewport, not on
the guest screen, so they send no touch.

DefWindowProc's WM_NCLBUTTONDOWN(HTCAPTION) activates and raises the window,
so the WM_MOUSEACTIVATE answer for the caption (ModAVD#160) is not needed
any more and goes.

Refs ModAVD/ModAVD#161

